    "rule": "For every KILL cell, max_absolute_error must be >= kill_margin_factor * tolerances.output with kill_margin_factor = 10 (declared here, not tuned). For every INACTIVE cell, max_absolute_error must be <= tolerances.output. A cell that violates either rule FAILS the test and is reported; expectations are not relabelled to observed output. WEAK_STRUCTURAL cells require tolerance < max_absolute_error < kill_margin_factor * tolerance.",
    "reasoning": "For hc_mult H=2, three Sinkhorn rounds converge comb to a doubly-stochastic 2x2 matrix, which is necessarily symmetric [[a,1-a],[1-a,a]]; transposing it is nearly the identity, so the transpose mutant is detectable only through the residual non-convergence (8.3e-4). This is a property of the geometry, not of the fixture values. The two cells are declared WEAK_STRUCTURAL: observed error must lie strictly between tolerance and the margin (evidence that the mutant is detectable but weakly), and the mutant class must be strongly killed on a new H=3 fixture where comb is not symmetric (oracle asymmetry 0.0707). The same mechanism applies to hc-axis-initial: for a 2x2 matrix, alternating row/column normalisation over three rounds converges to the same doubly-stochastic fixed point regardless of which axis is normalised first, so flipping the initial axis leaves only the convergence residual (9.1e-4). On the H=3 fixture the same mutant kills at 1.9e-1. The v2 misprediction is preserved as evidence that the matrix is derived, not fitted; v3 corrects the model of the mechanism, not the observations."
  }
